Subject: [PATCH 3/4] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's patch 3, built on
 ea41e6fc9): A GATE'S DAY IS THE COMMIT'S, NOT THE CLOCK'S (the seat's planted
 case PV15). Run at the commit's own date and sixty days on, lead-debt,
 point-debt and relay-debt changed their answer: their checkers, and
 harvest-debt's, aged the rows against the wall clock, and the variable that
 moves the date for a test moved it for the gate as well. The day is now
 decided in one place, scripts/checks/commit_day.py, in one order: --today DAY
 on the command line, which each of the four gates passes with the date of
 HEAD's commit, so nothing in the environment can move a gate's day; then the
 seam OA_<NAME>_TODAY, kept for a test and for a look ahead by hand, read only
 when a checker is run without --today; then the date of HEAD's commit. Never
 the wall clock; with no day given and no commit to read, a refusal; and a day
 that is not a day (--today tomorrow) is refused in plain words where it was
 answered with a traceback. point-debt, which now needs the commit, joins the
 table of gates that reach git. Two of main's tests pinned the wall clock
 ('the clock is real') and now pin the commit's day, each with a dated note.
 The lock: three tests added to
 tests/test_gates_are_a_function_of_the_commit.py; on the commit before, the
 first fails with the three gates by name and the second finds the four reads
 of the clock. 0 of 19.

